A hostile, sacrificial cult in Level 1 consists of roughly 50 members who are aggressive toward anyone not belonging to their religious group.

Wanderer logs from Level 1 describe a mysterious being in a tattered suit with five eyes that has been known to kill Hounds in the dark.

A friendly colony of 30 to 60 people owned by the Backroom Colonists is stationed in Level 1 and is known for being open to trade.

Almond Water is a naturally flavored beverage is the most abundant source of hydration in the Backrooms, featuring a sweet taste of almond and vanilla mixed with rose water.

Instances of Almond Water manifest completely at random in unoccupied spaces or behind objects, as they are never observed appearing in a location while someone is watching.

Consuming Almond Water can cure minor illnesses, improve overall focus, and neutralize the negative psychological or physical effects inflicted by hostile entities.

If not kept fresh or refrigerated, Almond Water will grow white, blue, or green mold and smell like rotten cheese, becoming a poisonous liquid that causes internal bleeding and dementia.

Beyond the common clear liquid, rare colored versions of Almond Water exist, such as the Blue variant which rinses away insanity or the Red variant which drastically speeds up wound healing.

there are infinite different levels with infinite different looks, functions, and entities. 

The Blurring is a poorly-understood mental hazard that primarily affects wanderers in extremely dark places like the Void, causing them to feel as if they are "blinking and being somewhere else" with no memory of the transition.

Memories associated with the Blurring phenomenon are notoriously distorted and inconsistent, frequently featuring strange motifs of shattering that make it nearly impossible for the afflicted to recall specific details.

Individuals actively undergoing the Blurring exhibit an increased tolerance for physical pain and a profound degree of emotional detachment from the world around them.

While the duration of the Blurring is unpredictable, the longest recorded instance lasted over two months and resulted in the victim waking up with severe paranoia and bleeding from the eyes.

The Blurring often serves as an involuntary transport system, depositing wanderers in entirely unrelated locations such as Level 139 or various "dead-end" levels that are otherwise difficult to access.

The Broken is a fragmented, corrupted expanse located outside the standard realm of the Backrooms that serves as a proverbial "dumping ground" for discarded data and deleted levels.

The landscape of The Broken is a chaotic, kaleidoscopic mess of polychromatic light and shifting geometric shapes that lack any logical spatial structure.

Physical objects within The Broken are ephemeral constructs devoid of substance, existing only as temporary accretions that lose their identity as they drift toward the horizon.

A deafening, discordant cacophony of non-sinusoidal tones and howling frequencies permeates the environment of The Broken, creating a constant state of auditory distress.

The fabric of reality in The Broken is so splintered that light itself falls into distortion, creating refractions that seem to emanate from everywhere and nowhere simultaneously.

The Broken is theorized to be the source of much of the hostile and unstable energy found within the main Backrooms due to its nature as a messy conglomeration of ripped-apart dimensions.

Making direct physical contact with the fragmented surfaces of The Broken can rupture a traveler's being by overwriting them with a sudden torrent of corrupted data.

Higher-dimensional objects frequently phase through the contorted terrain of The Broken, and touching them is reported to tear a wanderer apart on a molecular level.

Conventional entities do not inhabit The Broken, but prismatic objects that phase in and out of the level often vaguely resemble multiple different creatures merged into a single form.

Any entity introduced to the environment of The Broken will eventually take on its qualities, transmuting into shimmering blocks of color before merging permanently with the landscape.

Some researchers believe The Broken was once a massive energy source used for the creation of life in the Backrooms before it collapsed into its current entropic state.

The Broken shares a symbiotic relationship with the Backrooms, acting as a chaotic singularity where information that is no longer consciously perceived goes to fade away.

Entering The Broken is exceptionally difficult and usually requires a severely haphazard or "glitched" method of noclipping that bypasses standard dimensional barriers.

There are no known frames of reference for the true extent of The Broken, as its shifting configurations of data extend infinitely into a polychromatic unknown.

It is hypothesized that if the proper methods were discovered, the unstable nature of The Broken could function as a means of instantaneous transportation to any location in the Backrooms.

Hounds are hostile canid entities that inhabit sparsely populated, damp, and derelict regions of the Backrooms.

Despite their beastly appearance, Hounds are genetically similar to humans and retain certain hominid biotic attributes.

Hounds exhibit enough critical thinking to use rudimentary tools like sticks and rocks for foraging or self-cleaning.

The unique pelt of a Hound is a highly sought-after resource because it is one of the only available sources of leather in the Backrooms.

Hounds possess powerful jaw strength and rows of sharp teeth used to tear off large chunks of flesh from their prey.

While they have sharp claws, Hounds often wear them dull by obsessively scratching at their own faces until the bone is visible.

Hounds have an incredibly acute sense of hearing, allowing them to detect a wanderer from up to two miles away.

A sensitive response to light known as positive phototaxis makes Hounds prefer staying in illuminated areas rather than complete darkness.

Hounds are incapable of speech because the infection process destroys the entity’s speech center, leaving them only able to bark or whine.

The gleaming white eyes of a Hound are a distinct identifier that can be spotted from a great distance in low-light environments.

Egremond Syndrome is the rabies-like neurotropic virus responsible for facilitating the rapid DNA mutation of a human into a Hound.

Initial symptoms of Egremond Syndrome include heavy migraines, lightheadedness, and jaundice-like discoloration around the site of a bite.

Egremond Syndrome causes a mange-like skin disease to spread rapidly around the wound before the full transformation begins.

The transformation caused by Egremond Syndrome is excruciating, as human teeth and nails are painfully pushed out and replaced by fangs and claws.

During the Egremond Syndrome mutation, the victim’s skeletal structure is reconstructed to force them to move exclusively on all fours.

Egremond Syndrome causes the eyes to lose all pigmentation and the skin to thin, giving the resulting Hound a signature bony appearance.

Within 20 to 30 minutes of the first symptoms appearing, Egremond Syndrome will have completely reconstructed the victim's body.

A newly transformed victim of Egremond Syndrome enters a month-long phase of hyper-aggression and agility before settling into standard Hound behavior.

Dousing a bite wound in Almond Water within five minutes is a known method to neutralize the pathogen of Egremond Syndrome.

If Egremond Syndrome remains in the body for more than ten minutes without treatment, neutralizing the victim is the only recommended course of action.

In addition to Almond Water, bloodletting and electroshock therapy are documented, though extreme, treatments for Egremond Syndrome.

Egremond Syndrome was named by a pharmacist who discovered the virus while studying the salivary glands of deceased Hound specimens.
